  "body": [
    "The dashboard reads as a rates-and-dollar squeeze driven by inflation pressure, with growth still intact. The 10-year yield is at 5.31%, a 52-week high, up 53 basis points over the month and 115 on the year; the 30-year is at its high too. The dollar index is at a 52-week high, up 3% over the month. Brent at $100, up 65% year-to-date, is the inflation impulse the bond market is pricing against. Yet this is no flight from risk: copper sits at 89% of its 52-week range, up 18% this year, and bitcoin is up 34% over three months.",
    "The relationships settle it. The 30y–10y spread has flattened 11 basis points over the month with both legs rising, a bear flattening that says the market is pricing a tighter policy path rather than a long-run boom. Gold down 7% over the month while yields climb is the real-yield story, not fear. The copper/gold ratio up 9% over the month, and bitcoin buying 13% more gold than a month ago, say risk appetite has survived the rate shock.",
    "The tension is dollar versus oil. USD/CAD at a 52-week high with crude at $100 is unusual; the loonie normally rides oil. That the dollar wins anyway says rate differentials, not commodities, are steering currencies, so the bond market carries the most weight. Brent's 2% drop today, in a choppy regime, is noise against its 40% three-month gain."
